I'm locking this thread subject, which means that people can start new =
threads, but messages will be held if it replies to this one.

spt
(for the chairs)

> On Oct 15, 2026, at 19:02, Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> wrote:
>=20
> In response to your message: =
https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/G3WsjtrcMruuYdws5m-VcBKoOmk
>=20
> This matter concerns not only the specific document in question but =
also systemic deficiencies within the Working Group.
>=20
> There is no reason why procedural irregularities within a Working =
Group should not be debated openly and directly within that very group, =
especially to ensure full transparency. If IETF promises were taken =
seriously, such discussions should take place freely.
>=20
> It seems that framing criticism within bureaucratic formalities serves =
as a mechanism to silence dissent within the TLS Working Group (and =
similarly in the SSH Working Group). Furthermore, the decision-making =
bodies appear to be dominated by large U.S. corporations whose interests =
largely align with those of the NSA, suggesting that the outcomes of =
these decisions are predetermined. This appears to be an established =
tactic to exhaust the resources and morale of dissenters.
>=20
> The IETF profile page indicates that you currently hold no active =
roles [1], yet Professor Bernstein notes a reference to a (possibly =
former) "NSA contractor Eric Rescorla" [2].
>=20
> Are these comments a reflection of your personal views, or do they =
represent an official or semi-official stance on behalf of the NSA, =
given your history as a (possibly former) NSA contractor?
>=20
> How do you propose we address this conflict of interest?
>=20
> Responses typically attempt to deflect attention from the obvious =
conflict of interest or advocate for the suppression of dissent. To =
date, I have observed no credible attempts to address this issue within =
the IETF framework.
>=20
> Kind regards,
>=20
> Ken Kubota
>=20
> ____________________________________________________
>=20
> Ken Kubota
> https://doi.org/10.4444/100
>=20
>=20
> [1] https://datatracker.ietf.org/person/Eric%20Rescorla
> "Eric does not currently have any active roles."
>=20
> [2] https://blog.cr.yp.to/20260702-standard.html
>=20
> _______________________________________________
